/// eo9:net-rtl8125 — the RTL8125 2.5GbE driver world (plan/09; the board lane of
/// plan/12's Orange Pi 5 Plus bring-up).
///
/// `net.rtl8125` is `net.virtio`'s real-silicon sibling: it imports the PCI capability
/// (`eo9:pci/pci`), drives a Realtek RTL8125 PCIe NIC (the two onboard 2.5GbE ports of
/// the Orange Pi 5 Plus enumerate as 10ec:8125 behind the RK3588's DW root ports)
/// through configuration space, BAR registers, and DMA descriptor rings, and exports
/// `eo9:net/l2` — interfaces, MAC addresses, whole Ethernet frames — so the existing
/// link-layer stack (`l2check`, `net.l4.over-l2`, `net.text`, telnetd's session
/// composition) runs over the physical wire by composition: `net.rtl8125 $ program`.
///
/// `eo9:text/text` is imported only for one diagnostic line on first use (the probed
/// MAC address and negotiated link speed); the driver works identically without a
/// console.
///
/// There is no configure interface: the documented default behaviour is to claim the
/// first RTL8125 function visible through the granted PCI capability on first use
/// (the option-C default-configuration rule, plan/09 Decision 14). Selecting one of
/// the two board NICs is `pci.filtered`'s job, composed in front of the driver
/// (`pci.admit-address` distinguishes them — both NICs share the 10ec:8125 identity,
/// so the vendor policy cannot).
package eo9:net-rtl8125@0.1.0;

world rtl8125-net {
    import eo9:pci/pci@0.1.0;
    import eo9:text/text@0.1.0;

    export eo9:net/l2@0.1.0;
}
